==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈseːon/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*sehwaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*sekʷ-||to see, notice}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|sīa|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian ''[[sjen]]''), Old Saxon ''[[sehan]]'' (Low German {{term|sehn|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch {{term|sian|lang=odt}} (Dutch ''[[zien]]''), Old High German ''[[sehan]]'' (German ''[[sehen]]''), Old Norse {{term|sjá|lang=non}} (Danish and Swedish {{term|se|lang=se}}, Icelandic {{term|sjá|lang=is}}), Gothic {{term|𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽|tr=saihwan|lang=got}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon|type=strong|class=V}}

# to [[see]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=strong|V|sēo|siehst|siehþ|sēoþ|sēo|sēon|seah|sāwe, sǣġe|seah|sāwon, sǣgon|sāwe, sǣġe|sāwen, sǣġen|seoh|sēoþ|sēonde|(ġe)sewen, (ġe)seġen}}

=====Descendants=====
* Middle English: {{l|enm|seen}}
** Scots: {{l|sco|se}}, {{l|sco|sie}}
** English: [[see]]

===Etymology 2===
Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|sīn||to be|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|sum||to be|lang=la}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*h₁es-}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|type=irreg|head=sēon}}

# To [[be]], used primarily in reference to God
#: '' Fæder ūser, þū sē '''eart''' on heofonum, '''sī''' þīn nama gehālgod '' &ndash; Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=irreg||eom|(eart)|is|sind(on), sint · (earon)|sīe|sīen|(wæs)|(wǣre)|(wæs)|(wǣron)|(wǣre)|(wǣren)|sī, sēo|''unattested''|(wesende)|''unattested''}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*sīganą}}, {{term/t|gem-pro|*sīhwaną||to strain, drip}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*seyk-||to pour, strain}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon|type=strong|class=I}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=ang}} to [[strain]], [[filter]]
# {{context|intransitive|lang=ang}} to [[run]] as a sore, ooze, [[trickle]], [[drop]], [[drip]]

